{"type": "step", "node_id": 23, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this problem, I need to find EN² where E is the intersection of legs BC and DA, and N is the second intersection of circumcircles of △BMC and △DMA.\n\n**Setting up coordinates.** Place CD on the x-axis: C = (0,0), D = (10,0), so M = (5,0). Since AB ∥ CD with AB = 5, let A = (a, h), B = (b, h) with a = b + 5 (A near D, B near C).\n\n**Finding the vertices.** From DA = 7: (a−10)² + h² = 49 → (b−5)² + h² = 49. From BC = 9: b² + h² = 81. Subtracting: (b−5)² − b² = −32 → −10b + 25 = −32 → b = 57/10, so a = 107/10. Then h² = 81 − (57/10)² = 4851/100 = 441·11/100, giving h = 21√11/10.\n\nSo: B = (57/10, 21√11/10), A = (107/10, 21√11/10).\n\n**Finding E (intersection of BC and DA).** Line BC: (57t/10, ht). Line DA: (10 + 7s/10, hs). From y-coordinates t = s; from x: 57t/10 = 10 + 7t/10 → 5t = 10 → t = 2. Thus **E = (57/5, 21√11/5)**.\n\n**Circumcircle of BMC.** Equation x² + y² + Dx + Ey + F = 0. Through C=(0,0): F = 0. Through M=(5,0): D = −5. Through B: (57/10)² + h² − 5·(57/10) + Eh = 0 → 81 − 28.5 + Eh = 0 → E = −52.5/h = −25√11/11.\n\n**Circle₁:** x² + y² − 5x − (25√11/11)y = 0.\n\n**Circumcircle of DMA.** Through D=(10,0) and M=(5,0): D' = −15, F' = 50. Through A: (107/10)² + h² − 15·(107/10) + E'h + 50 = 0 → 163 − 160.5 + E'h + 50 = 0 → E' = −25√11/11.\n\n**Circle₂:** x² + y² − 15x − (25√11/11)y + 50 = 0.\n\n**Finding N.** Subtracting Circle₁ from Circle₂: −10x + 50 = 0 → **x = 5**. Substituting into Circle₁: 25 + y² − 25 − (25√11/11)y = 0 → y(y − 25√11/11) = 0. The solution y = 0 is M; the other is **N = (5, 25√11/11)**.\n\n**Computing EN².**\n\n$$EN^2 = \\left(\\frac{57}{5} - 5\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{21\\sqrt{11}}{5} - \\frac{25\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{106\\sqrt{11}}{55}\\right)^2$$\n\n$$= \\frac{1024}{25} + \\frac{106^2 \\cdot 11}{55^2} = \\frac{1024}{25} + \\frac{123596}{3025} = \\frac{11264}{275} + \\frac{11236}{275} = \\frac{22500}{275} = \\frac{900}{11}$$\n\nSince gcd(900, 11) = 1, we have a = 900, b = 11.\n\n$$100a + b = 90000 + 11 = \\boxed{90011}$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this geometry problem. Let me set up coordinates.\n\nTrapezoid ABCD with AB ∥ CD, AB=5, BC=9, CD=10, DA=7. Lines BC and DA intersect at E. M is midpoint of CD. N is the other intersection of circumcircles of BMC and DMA. Find EN² = a/b, compute 100a+b.\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Place CD on x-axis. Let C = (0,0), D = (10,0). M = (5,0) midpoint of CD.\n\nAB ∥ CD, AB = 5. Let A = (a, h) and B = (b, h) for some height h. Since AB = 5, |b - a| = 5. \n\nDA = 7: distance from D=(10,0) to A=(a,h): (a-10)² + h² = 49.\nBC = 9: distance from B=(b,h) to C=(0,0): b² + h² = 81.\n\nAlso AB ∥ CD means both at height h. AB = 5 so |b-a| = 5.\n\nFor a trapezoid with AB ∥ CD, AB on top (shorter). Let's say A is above D and B is above C, so a > b. Actually let me think: A connects to D, B connects to C. So A is near D, B is near C. So a is close to 10, b is close to 0. b - a = -5, so a = b + 5.\n\nDA: (a-10)² + h² = 49 → (b+5-10)² + h² = 49 → (b-5)² + h² = 49.\nBC: b² + h² = 81.\n\nSubtract: (b-5)² - b² = 49 - 81 = -32.\nb² - 10b + 25 - b² = -32.\n-10b + 25 = -32.\n-10b = -57.\nb = 57/10 = 5.7.\n\na = 5.7 + 5 = 10.7.\n\nh² = 81 - b² = 81 - 32.49 = 48.51 = 4851/100.\nh = √(4851/100) = √4851/10. 4851 = 9·539 = 9·7·77 = 9·7·7·11 = 9·49·11 = 441·11. So h = 21√11/10.\n\nLet me verify DA: (a-10)² + h² = (0.7)² + 48.51 = 0.49 + 48.51 = 49. ✓\n\nSo:\nC = (0,0), D = (10,0), M = (5,0).\nB = (57/10, 21√11/10), A = (107/10, 21√11/10).\n\nLet me use fractions. Let h = 21√11/10.\n\nB = (57/10, h), A = (107/10, h).\n\nNow find E = intersection of lines BC and DA.\n\nLine BC: through C=(0,0) and B=(57/10, h). Parametric: (t·57/10, t·h).\nLine DA: through D=(10,0) and A=(107/10, h). Direction: A - D = (107/10 - 10, h) = (7/10, h). Parametric: (10 + s·7/10, s·h).\n\nSet equal: t·57/10 = 10 + s·7/10, t·h = s·h → t = s.\n\nSo t·57/10 = 10 + t·7/10 → t·(57-7)/10 = 10 → t·50/10 = 10 → t·5 = 10 → t = 2.\n\nE = (2·57/10, 2·h) = (114/10, 2h) = (57/5, 42√11/10) = (57/5, 21√11/5).\n\nSo E = (57/5, 21√11/5).\n\nNow find circumcircle of BMC and circumcircle of DMA, find their second intersection N.\n\nCircumcircle of BMC: B = (57/10, h), M = (5, 0), C = (0, 0).\n\nSince M and C are both on x-axis, the circle through M, C, B. Let me find its equation.\n\nGeneral circle: x² + y² + Dx + Ey + F = 0.\n\nThrough C=(0,0): F = 0.\nThrough M=(5,0): 25 + 5D = 0 → D = -5.\nThrough B=(57/10, h): (57/10)² + h² + (-5)(57/10) + E·h = 0.\n\n(57/10)² = 3249/100. h² = 4851/100. Sum = 8100/100 = 81.\n-5·(57/10) = -285/10 = -28.5 = -2850/100.\n\nSo 81 - 28.5 + E·h = 0 → 52.5 + E·h = 0 → E = -52.5/h = -52.5/(21√11/10) = -525/(21√11) = -25/√11 = -25√11/11.\n\nCircle 1 (BMC): x² + y² - 5x - (25√11/11)y = 0.\n\nCircumcircle of DMA: D = (10,0), M = (5,0), A = (107/10, h).\n\nThrough D=(10,0): 100 + 10D' + F' = 0.\nThrough M=(5,0): 25 + 5D' + F' = 0.\nSubtract: 75 + 5D' = 0 → D' = -15. F' = -100 - 10(-15) = -100 + 150 = 50.\n\nThrough A=(107/10, h): (107/10)² + h² - 15·(107/10) + E'·h + 50 = 0.\n\n(107/10)² = 11449/100. h² = 4851/100. Sum = 16300/100 = 163.\n-15·107/10 = -1605/10 = -160.5.\nSo 163 - 160.5 + E'·h + 50 = 0 → 52.5 + E'·h = 0 → E' = -52.5/h = -25√11/11.\n\nInteresting, same E coefficient!\n\nCircle 2 (DMA): x² + y² - 15x - (25√11/11)y + 50 = 0.\n\nNow find intersections of the two circles. Subtract Circle 1 from Circle 2:\n\n(Circle 2) - (Circle 1): (-15x + 5x) + 50 = 0 → -10x + 50 = 0 → x = 5.\n\nSo both intersections have x = 5. One is M = (5, 0). The other N has x = 5.\n\nPlug x = 5 into Circle 1: 25 + y² - 25 - (25√11/11)y = 0 → y² - (25√11/11)y = 0 → y(y - 25√11/11) = 0.\n\ny = 0 (that's M) or y = 25√11/11.\n\nSo N = (5, 25√11/11).\n\nNow compute EN². E = (57/5, 21√11/5), N = (5, 25√11/11).\n\nEN² = (57/5 - 5)² + (21√11/5 - 25√11/11)².\n\n57/5 - 5 = 57/5 - 25/5 = 32/5.\n\n21√11/5 - 25√11/11 = √11·(21/5 - 25/11) = √11·(231/55 - 125/55) = √11·(106/55) = 106√11/55.\n\nEN² = (32/5)² + (106√11/55)² = 1024/25 + (106²·11)/(55²).\n\n106² = 11236. 11236·11 = 123596. 55² = 3025.\n\n123596/3025. Let me simplify. GCD(123596, 3025). 3025 = 25·121 = 5²·11². 123596 = 11236·11 = (106²)·11. 106 = 2·53. 106² = 4·2809. So 123596 = 4·2809·11. 2809 = 53². So 123596 = 4·53²·11.\n\n3025 = 25·121 = 5²·11².\n\nGCD = 11. 123596/11 = 11236. 3025/11 = 275.\n\nSo 123596/3025 = 11236/275.\n\nNow 1024/25 = 1024·11/275 = 11264/275.\n\nEN² = 11264/275 + 11236/275 = 22500/275.\n\n22500/275. GCD(22500, 275). 275 = 25·11. 22500 = 225·100 = 9·25·100 = 9·2500. 22500/25 = 900. So 22500 = 25·900. 275 = 25·11. GCD = 25. 22500/25 = 900. 275/25 = 11.\n\nEN² = 900/11.\n\na = 900, b = 11. 100a + b = 90000 + 11 = 90011.\n\nLet me double-check the computation of E and N.\n\nE: lines BC and DA. \nLine BC direction from C: B - C = (57/10, h). Parametric C + t(B-C) = (57t/10, ht).\nLine DA direction from D: A - D = (7/10, h). Parametric D + s(A-D) = (10 + 7s/10, hs).\nt = s from y. 57t/10 = 10 + 7t/10 → 50t/10 = 10 → 5t = 10 → t = 2. E = (114/10, 2h) = (57/5, 2h). h = 21√11/10, so 2h = 21√11/5. ✓\n\nN = (5, 25√11/11). ✓\n\nEN² = (32/5)² + (106√11/55)².\n\nLet me recompute the y-difference: 21√11/5 - 25√11/11.\nCommon denominator 55: 21·11/55 - 25·5/55 = 231/55 - 125/55 = 106/55. ✓\n\n(106√11/55)² = 106²·11/55² = 11236·11/3025 = 123596/3025.\n\nSimplify: 123596/3025. 123596 = 11·11236. 3025 = 11·275. So = 11236/275. 11236 = 4·2809 = 4·53². 275 = 25·11. GCD(11236, 275): 275 = 5²·11. 11236 = 2²·53². No common factors. So 11236/275. ✓\n\n1024/25 = 1024·11/(25·11) = 11264/275. ✓\n\nSum = (11264 + 11236)/275 = 22500/275.\n\n22500/275: 22500 = 2²·3²·5⁴·... let me factor. 22500 = 225·100 = 9·25·100 = 9·2500 = 9·2²·5⁴ = 2²·3²·5⁴. 275 = 5²·11. GCD = 5² = 25. 22500/25 = 900 = 2²·3²·5². 275/25 = 11. \n\nEN² = 900/11. GCD(900,11) = 1. ✓\n\n100a + b = 90000 + 11 = 90011.\n\nThe answer is 90011.", "tool_calls": [],